<title>SWEDEN: Volvo Belgian plant to hold Friday stoppage.</title>
<headline>Volvo Belgian plant to hold Friday stoppage.</headline>
<dateline>STOCKHOLM 1997-03-06</dateline>
<text>
<p>Swedish vehicle maker AB Volvo said on Thursday workers at its car plant in Gent, Belgium, will strike for two hours on Friday in sympathy with colleagues at Renault's plant in Vilvoorde which will be closed.</p>
<p>&quot;The whole car industry in Belgium will strike on Friday,&quot; Volvo spokesman Ingmar Hesslefors told Reuters.</p>
<p>&quot;As far as we are concerned, the strike encompasses one hour per shift, which means a total of two hours.&quot;</p>
<p>Hesslefors said the strike means Volvo will lose production of 90 cars, out of a total daily production of 700 cars.</p>
<p>&quot;As an outside observer you can understand the reaction in Belgium,&quot; he said.</p>
<p>Renault announced last week that it would close the Vilvoorde plant which has 3,100 employees.</p>
<p>-- Birgitta Forsberg, Stockholm newsroom +46-8-700 1006</p>
